 @rrekki9320   Yep still is worth it if you check the latest review from Hardware Unboxed /watch?v=gt9h5ckS_xo a reference model (RX 5700 XT) with latest drivers  makes look bad a RTX 2070 Super <--- that is much more expensive (at least where I live). Now you must consider that Nitro+ is already overclocked.

I wish you good good luck with your new card. Sapphire has 2 years warranty (but you need to apply it directly with the vendor) and that could be a pain.

Many people complains about AMD drivers but I didn't have one (Well months ago with RDR2 on ultra snow was like blocks) but switching to DX12 fixed the issue. After a driver update problem gone.

 @coconutoil1614  Right now I'm having no trouble running some January 2020 release drivers, since I had some issues with newer ones with games like COD Warzone (graphics looked aweful). The only thing that bugs me is a very occasionally and random black scren while gaming, but I solve it reconnecting the HDMI cable to the card and signal instantly returns.
I should try the latest drivers, but so far I'm good with this ones.

So if you're wondering if this is a goos buy, I'd say it actually is, considering the money. It's a great card, apart from the sofware that runs it. Still, if you have the money to go for a 2070 super, then go for that one, which has slightly better performance and drivers. For me that was not an option, since in my country costs almost double.

 @givemeajackson  
I think that the MSRP includes some tax, it is the recommended price for shops and even in the USA people have to pay tax over their purchases in most states. Regardless, much more interesting is to compare the prices between Europe and the USA for tenths of products, when for the 3700X, 3600, 2700X, Asus Prime X470, bloody reference 5700 XT with its crappy blower cooler...the price in € is roughly the price in USD and for other cards/motherboards... you can add 20% then the problem is not the VAT or whatever, the problem is a lack of supply and a crappy distribution chain for those companies. We always have this problem with Sapphire and AMD-cards, we always are the first to have higher prices or non-deliverable cards when there is a supply issue. 

That brings me to the second point, we still can't buy the Red Devil 5700 XT, we can only pre-order it. That was as much a paper launch as I have ever seen. The Nitro 5700 XT is not even listed yet. There are more cards for which you can't buy those yet or have an uncertain (10+ days) delivery time. There is a supply-chain problem!

 @givemeajackson  

Once again, the MSRP is irrelevant in this context, the hard proof comes from the price-comparison between the USA and Europe for tenths of products. When most produts have the same price in € as in USD (Newegg and Amazon) and some don't then there is a supply issue. Don't look at the MSRP, compare prices for tenths of products between the USA and wherever you live, that is how you know which prices are too high. 


In this particular case it is very easy. The reference 5700 XT with its horrible blower cooler costs €410, the Sapphire Pulse €480. The difference in MSRP is only $10, $410 vs. $400. You see the problem? A $10 higher MSRP 'translates' to a €10 higher price in the shops here, but we don't see it. Yes, it could be €11 or 12, not €70!

